# Target C — exclude the Section-146 Type-II endpoint by outer direction measure

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only endpoint exclusion; theorem authority unchanged.  
**Dependencies:** audited-working §§142 and 146, the audited §146 face-rank endpoint-stability algebra for `k,Q-f,b-per(F)`, and canonical Type-II near-disk convergence.

Put

\[
\Delta_2:={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
s:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\]

and let

\[
\boxed{
C_{146}:={4\over9}(\pi+s)
=1.8195135379794445\ldots .
}
\tag{1}
\]

Audited-working §146 gives the Type-II lower bound

\[
\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge C_{146}
\]

after retaining its defect terms. This note proves that the endpoint itself is not
attainable:

> **Theorem.** No unbounded canonical Type-II C-II survivor sequence can satisfy
> \[
> {q-k\over D}\longrightarrow C_{146}.
> \tag{2}
> \]

The mechanism is different from the older exclusion of the obsolete
`(2/5)(pi+sqrt(Delta_2))` endpoint. At the §146 endpoint, the complete train graph is
not globally near-Mantel. Instead its **atomic-phase subgraph** is exactly at Mantel
capacity, while the non-atomic trains saturate the loaded-hull exposure inequality.
This forces a positive linear number of outer-walk edge occurrences onto only three
projective train axes. But an occurrence-counted walk of asymptotically minimal length
around a near disk has a unique limiting edge-direction measure: uniform Lebesgue
measure on the projective circle. A positive atomic part is impossible.

No source point is deleted and no contact-isolated coordinate enters the Section-57
core rigidity theorem.

---

## 1. Scalar endpoint consequences

Write

\[
t_D:={q-k\over D}.
\]

Assume (2). The audited §146 endpoint-stability algebra gives

\[
\boxed{{k\over D}\to0,}
\tag{3}
\]

\[
\boxed{{Q-f\over D}\to0,}
\tag{4}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{{b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}\to0.}
\tag{5}
\]

Since Type II has

\[
{\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}\to\pi,
\]

(5) gives

\[
\boxed{{b\over D}\to\pi.}
\tag{6}
\]

Also `q/D=t_D+k/D`, hence

\[
\boxed{{q\over D}\to C_{146}.}
\tag{7}
\]

We next recover the exact face and train densities at this endpoint. Audited-working
§146 gives

\[
{f\over D}
\ge s-{1\over4}{h\over D}
+{1\over4}{d_H\over D}
+{1\over2}{s_Z\over D}-o(1).
\tag{8}
\]

Because `k=d_H+s_Z=o(D)` and `h-s_Z=q-k`,

\[
{h\over D}=C_{146}+o(1).
\]

Thus

\[
\liminf {f\over D}\ge s-{C_{146}\over4}.
\tag{9}
\]

The exact outer ledger gives `Q/D<=2t_D-pi+o(1)`, while `Q-f=o(D)`. Therefore

\[
\limsup {f\over D}\le2C_{146}-\pi.
\tag{10}
\]

The two constants are equal because

\[
2C_{146}-\pi
=s-{C_{146}\over4}
={8s-\pi\over9}.
\]

Set

\[
\boxed{
f_0:={8s-\pi\over9}.}
\tag{11}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{{f\over D}\to f_0,\qquad {Q\over D}\to f_0.}
\tag{12}
\]

The exact stopped-train ledger is

\[
T=q+2f-k.
\tag{13}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
{T\over D}\to
x:=C_{146}+2f_0
={C_{146}\over2}+2s.
}
\tag{14}
\]

In particular

\[
\boxed{
x-{C_{146}\over2}=2s.}
\tag{15}
\]

This is exactly the equality value in the pre-elimination phase inequality of §142.

---

## 2. Equality in the §142 atomic/non-atomic split

For the empirical projective train-phase measures, pass to the subsequence used in
§142. Let `mu` be a weak limit, let

\[
a:=\text{total atomic mass of }\mu,
\]

and put

\[
y:={C_{146}\over x}
=\lim {q\over T}.
\]

The two key inequalities proved in §142 are

\[
\boxed{2s\le xa}
\tag{16}
\]

from the quadratic strict-rhombus crossing measure, and

\[
\boxed{y\le2(1-a)}
\tag{17}
\]

from loaded-hull exposure.

Equation (17) gives

\[
xa\le x-{C_{146}\over2}.
\]

By (15), the right side is exactly `2s`. Thus (16)–(17) are both equalities:

\[
\boxed{xa=2s,}
\tag{18}
\]

\[
\boxed{x(1-a)={C_{146}\over2}.}
\tag{19}
\]

So the endpoint has exactly

\[
(2s+o(1))D
\]

atomic-phase trains and

\[
\left({C_{146}\over2}+o(1)\right)D
\]

non-atomic trains.

---

## 3. Equality forces one three-axis atomic orbit

We record the equality case of the weighted Mantel step inside §142.

On the projective phase circle `R/pi Z`, two limiting train phases can support a
positive strict-rhombus crossing density only when their projective separation is
`pi/3`. The relation `phi -> phi+pi/3` has order three. Hence the phase-relation graph
decomposes into disjoint three-point orbits

\[
\{\alpha,\alpha+\pi/3,\alpha+2\pi/3\}.
\tag{20}
\]

Let `m_j` be the total atomic phase mass in one such orbit. The finite train-crossing
graph is triangle-free. On one three-point orbit, weighted Mantel gives crossing mass
at most `m_j^2/4`. Summing over the disjoint orbits gives total atomic crossing mass at
most

\[
{1\over4}\sum_jm_j^2
\le {1\over4}\left(\sum_jm_j\right)^2
={a^2\over4}.
\tag{21}
\]

At the present endpoint the normalized rhombus crossing density is

\[
{\Delta_2\over x^2}
={s^2\over x^2}
={a^2\over4}
\tag{22}
\]

by (18). Thus equality holds throughout (21).

Equality in

\[
\sum_jm_j^2\le\left(\sum_jm_j\right)^2
\]

forces all atomic mass into a single three-point orbit. Therefore there is an angle
`alpha` such that every atom of the limiting train-phase measure lies in

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal A
:=\{\alpha,\alpha+\pi/3,\alpha+2\pi/3\}
\pmod\pi.
}
\tag{23}
\]

Equivalently, after choosing a slowly shrinking radius `eta_D ->0`, all but `o(D)` of
the atomic-phase trains have projective contact direction within `eta_D` of
`mathcal A`.

This is the atomic-subgraph analogue of the three-axis collapse in the older global
near-Mantel endpoint theorem.

---

## 4. Loaded-hull exposure equality uses all non-atomic outer capacity

The proof of §142's inequality (17) is quantitative enough to retain its equality
case.

Fix a large route-length cutoff `M`, then small disjoint phase neighborhoods of the
three atoms in `mathcal A`. The §142 hull-exposure proof has two exceptional classes of
loaded-hull intervals:

1. intervals whose distinguished outer route has more than `M` edge occurrences;
   there are at most `2T/(M+1)` of these; and
2. short intervals all of whose train occurrences lie in the chosen atomic
   neighborhoods; near-disk perimeter-direction convergence bounds these by
   `O_M(eta D)+o_eta(D)`.

Every remaining loaded-hull interval contains an outer-face occurrence from a train
outside the atomic neighborhoods. The loaded-hull intervals are disjoint in the
occurrence-counted outer walk, so these are distinct outer occurrences.

First take `D->infinity`, then `eta->0`, then `M->infinity`, and diagonalize. Using
(19), one obtains shrinking neighborhoods `U_D` of `mathcal A` for which

\[
\boxed{
N_{out}^{nonatom}
\ge q-o(D)
=(C_{146}-o(1))D,
}
\tag{24}
\]

where `N_out^nonatom` counts outer-walk edge occurrences whose complete train phase
lies outside `U_D`.

On the other hand there are only

\[
\left({C_{146}\over2}+o(1)\right)D
\]

trains outside `U_D`, and every complete train has at most two outer terminal
occurrences. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
N_{out}^{nonatom}
=q+o(D).
}
\tag{25}
\]

Thus the non-atomic train population uses asymptotically **all** of its two-end outer
capacity. There is no linear non-atomic endpoint budget left for the defect faces.

Since the total outer-walk length satisfies `b=(pi+o(1))D`, equations (6) and (25)
give

\[
\boxed{
N_{out}^{atom}
:=b-N_{out}^{nonatom}
=(\pi-C_{146})D+o(D).
}
\tag{26}
\]

The coefficient is strictly positive:

\[
\pi-C_{146}
={5\pi-4s\over9}
=1.322079115610\ldots .
\tag{27}
\]

By construction, all but `o(D)` of the occurrences counted in (26) have projective
edge direction within `eta_D` of the fixed three-axis set `mathcal A`.

So the §146 endpoint forces a positive atomic part in the **outer-walk direction
measure**.

---

## 5. A perimeter-minimizing near-disk walk has uniform direction measure

We now prove the geometric obstruction in a form stronger than the three-axis special
case.

For one occurrence-counted outer contact walk, let `phi_e in R/pi Z` be the projective
direction of each unit edge occurrence and define the normalized direction measure

\[
\nu_D:={1\over D}\sum_{e\in W_{out}}\delta_{\phi_e}.
\tag{28}
\]

Its total mass is

\[
\nu_D(R/\pi Z)={b\over D}\to\pi.
\tag{29}
\]

For a projection direction `theta`, put

\[
V_D(\theta)
:=\sum_{e\in W_{out}}|\cos(\theta-\phi_e)|.
\tag{30}
\]

This is the total variation of the scalar projection of the closed outer walk. Since
the convex hull of the outer-walk vertices is exactly `F`,

\[
\boxed{V_D(\theta)\ge2w_F(\theta)}
\tag{31}
\]

for every `theta`, where `w_F(theta)` is the width of `F` in that direction.

Integrating over one projective period and using

\[
\int_0^\pi|\cos\theta|\,d\theta=2
\]

and Cauchy's perimeter formula

\[
\int_0^\pi w_F(\theta)\,d\theta=\operatorname{per}(F),
\]

we obtain

\[
\int_0^\pi
\bigl(V_D(\theta)-2w_F(\theta)\bigr)d\theta
=2\bigl(b-\operatorname{per}(F)\bigr)
=o(D).
\tag{32}
\]

The integrand is nonnegative. Type-II Hausdorff convergence to a diameter-one disk
gives, uniformly in `theta`,

\[
{w_F(\theta)\over D}\to1.
\tag{33}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{{V_D\over D}\to2\quad\text{in }L^1([0,\pi]).}
\tag{34}
\]

Pass to a weakly convergent subsequence `nu_D -> nu`. The measures have uniformly
bounded mass and the kernel

\[
K(t):=|\cos t|
\]

is continuous and Lipschitz on the projective circle. Hence

\[
{V_D(\theta)\over D}
=(K*\nu_D)(\theta)
\longrightarrow(K*\nu)(\theta)
\]

uniformly in `theta`. Comparing with (34),

\[
\boxed{K*\nu\equiv2.}
\tag{35}
\]

### Lemma 5.1 — injectivity of the projective cosine transform

If a finite measure `nu` on `R/pi Z` satisfies `|cos|*nu` constant, then `nu` is a
constant multiple of Lebesgue measure.

### Proof

On the period-`pi` circle,

\[
|\cos t|
={2\over\pi}
+{4\over\pi}
\sum_{n\ge1}{(-1)^{n+1}\over4n^2-1}\cos(2nt).
\tag{36}
\]

Every nonconstant Fourier coefficient in (36) is nonzero. Taking Fourier coefficients
of `K*nu=constant` therefore gives

\[
\widehat\nu(n)=0
\qquad(n\ne0).
\]

A finite measure on the circle with all nonzero Fourier coefficients zero is a
constant multiple of Lebesgue measure. `square`

By (29), the total mass of `nu` is `pi`, so the constant is one:

\[
\boxed{d\nu(\phi)=d\phi.}
\tag{37}
\]

In particular `nu` has no atoms.

---

## 6. Contradiction

Equation (26) says that for shrinking neighborhoods of the three fixed axes
`mathcal A`, the outer direction measure has mass

\[
\nu_D(U_D)
\ge\pi-C_{146}-o(1).
\]

After passing to the same subsequence used above,

\[
\boxed{
\nu(\mathcal A)
\ge\pi-C_{146}>0.
}
\tag{38}
\]

Thus `nu` has a positive atomic part supported on at most three directions.

Equation (37) says that `nu` is Lebesgue measure and has no atoms. Contradiction.

Therefore the assumed endpoint sequence (2) cannot exist. `square`

---

## 7. Consequence and exact scope

The proved statement is qualitative but strict:

\[
\boxed{
\text{No Type-II survivor sequence has }
(q-k)/D\to C_{146}.
}
\tag{39}
\]

Since audited-working §146 gives `liminf (q-k)/D >= C_146`, a standard diagonal
argument gives a positive separation at the level of possible Type-II subsequential
limits: hypothetical Type-II families whose limiting values approached `C_146` would
diagonalize to the forbidden endpoint sequence.

No explicit numerical separation constant is obtained, and no finite inequality of
the form

\[
q-k\ge(C_{146}+\epsilon_0)D-C
\]

is claimed.

This endpoint exclusion does **not** close Type II. Sequences with `(q-k)/D` bounded
away from `C_146` remain possible, as do the macroscopic inactive-container branches.

---

## 8. Hostile checklist

- **The endpoint is the live §146 endpoint, not the obsolete `1.63756...` floor.**
- **Atomic versus non-atomic trains:** only the atomic subgraph is Mantel-saturated;
  the full train graph is not assumed near-Mantel.
- **Three-axis conclusion:** equality in the atomic weighted-Mantel bound forces all
  atomic mass into one order-three `pi/3` phase orbit.
- **Hull-exposure equality:** the proof reuses the actual §142 interval-count argument,
  not the false implication that every outer edge is non-atomic.
- **Outer occurrence multiplicity:** `b`, `V_D`, and `nu_D` count physical outer-face
  occurrences, so bridges and repeated vertices are retained.
- **Direction-measure uniqueness:** the Fourier argument excludes any positive atomic
  component, not merely a pure three-axis measure.
- **Contact-isolated points:** untouched throughout.
- **Theorem authority:** unchanged; this is proof-only pending independent hostile
  review.

**Disposition:** proof-only exclusion of the Section-146 scalar endpoint. C-II and
Level I remain open.